  <title>7.3.2: Writing Equations for Patterns with Squares</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01777</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.3.2: Writing Equations for Patterns with Squares</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ca027aff-25be-4004-86c8-d1f131b7acd7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity </title>
<para><figure><media alt="Three steps of a growing pattern."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cdcca2de2d8650c3f470bc20faffa7b1228fea96"/></media></figure></para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> If the pattern continues, what will we see in Step 5 and Step 18? </para>
      <para> Sketch or describe the figure in each of these steps. </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Step 5 is a collection of 25 small squares, in a 5-by-5 arrangement, surrounded by 4 other small squares at the corners. Step 18 is a collection of \(18^2\) small squares, in an 18 by 18 arrangement, surrounded by 4 other small squares at the corners.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> How many small squares are in each of these steps? Explain how you know. </para>
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Step 5: 29, because it is \(5^2+4\) or \((5 \cdot 5)+4\). Step 18: 328, because it is \(18^2+4\) or \((18 \cdot 18)+4\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Write an equation to represent the relationship between the step number \(n\) and the number of squares \(y\). Be prepared to explain how each part of your equation relates to the pattern. (If you get stuck, try making a table.) </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(y=n^2+4\) or equivalent. The \(n^2\) term represents the number of small squares in the central \(n\)-by-\(n\) large square. The 4 represents the 4 small squares that are added to the large square at its 4 corners.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Sketch the first three steps of a pattern that can be represented by the equation \(y=n^2-1\). </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>An \(n\)-by-\(n\) array of squares with one square missing at the top right. The first step (for \(n=1\)) will have no squares.</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="DIAGRAM WITH 3 FIGURES SHOWING STEPS 1 THROUGH 3. STEP 1 DOES NOT SHOW ANYTHING. STEP 2 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 1 ROW AND 2 COLUMNS. 1 SQUARE IS ADDED TO THE TOP LEFT. STEP 3 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 2 ROWS AND 3 COLUMNS. 2 SQUARES ARE ADDED TO THE TOP LEFT."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8965a03887be419785389417c8ac9bb33490ead8"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
